      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127914,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 343,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is a band-like proliferation of small-calibre blood vessels lined by bland endothelium in the papillary and superficial reticular dermis, with no grenz zone and with a concomitant loss or decrease of elastic fibres in the affected area. Most cases exhibit a certain degree of hyperkeratosis or acanthosis and mild lymphocytic inflammation between the vessels. The differential diagnosis includes other vascular neoplasms, such as acquired elastotic haemangioma (smaller and with a similar pattern but with the presence of solar elastosis), microvenular haemangioma (involves the superficial and deep dermis, no absence of elastic fibres, and an absence of a plaque-like architecture), and hobnail haemangioma (wedge-shaped architecture, irregular lymphatic-like channels, lined by hobnail endothelial cells and haemosiderin deposits) {30930084}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:03:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
